1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,318
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper, presented at ICML 2024, introduces the
Distance Aware Bottleneck,</font>

2
00:00:05,398 --> 00:00:10,852
<font color="#FFFFFF">a new way to give deep neural networks the sense of
"knowing what they don't know." The</font>

3
00:00:10,932 --> 00:00:16,641
<font color="#FFFFFF">authors reframe uncertainty quantification as a
rate-distortion problem: the network learns</font>

4
00:00:16,721 --> 00:00:20,012
<font color="#FFFFFF">a compact codebook that summarizes its training data,</font>

5
00:00:20,092 --> 00:00:25,737
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the distance of a new input from that codebook
becomes a principled uncertainty score,</font>

6
00:00:25,817 --> 00:00:27,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">computed in a single forward pass.</font>

7
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:33,025
<font color="#FFFFFF">A trustworthy model should know when it is
operating far from what it has seen.</font>

8
00:00:33,105 --> 00:00:34,857
<font color="#FFFFFF">Deep neural networks, however,</font>

9
00:00:34,937 --> 00:00:40,903
<font color="#FFFFFF">often make confident predictions even on inputs
that are wildly different from their training data.</font>

10
00:00:40,983 --> 00:00:47,865
<font color="#FFFFFF">Classical probabilistic models such as Gaussian
Processes have a built-in sense of distance from the training set,</font>

11
00:00:47,945 --> 00:00:50,796
<font color="#FFFFFF">but standard deep networks do not, and reliable,</font>

12
00:00:50,876 --> 00:00:55,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">efficient uncertainty estimation for real
deployments remains an open problem.</font>

13
00:00:55,940 --> 00:01:00,105
<font color="#FFFFFF">Today's fast,</font>

14
00:01:00,185 --> 00:01:04,350
<font color="#FFFFFF">single-forward-pass uncertainty methods usually
depend on special architectural tricks,</font>

15
00:01:04,430 --> 00:01:08,595
<font color="#FFFFFF">like spectral normalization, to stop the network's
features from collapsing.</font>

16
00:01:08,675 --> 00:01:12,840
<font color="#FFFFFF">Those constraints can quietly damage calibration
and are awkward to bolt onto large pre-trained models.</font>

17
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:17,085
<font color="#FFFFFF">The alternative, deep ensembles and Bayesian networks,</font>

18
00:01:17,165 --> 00:01:21,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">are naturally distance-aware but require many
forward passes,</font>

19
00:01:21,410 --> 00:01:25,575
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is expensive at scale.</font>

20
00:01:25,655 --> 00:01:29,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors ask whether a single deterministic
model can be distance-aware without these drawbacks.</font>

21
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:36,093
<font color="#FFFFFF">Their key idea is to view uncertainty
quantification through the lens of rate-distortion theory.</font>

22
00:01:36,173 --> 00:01:41,506
<font color="#FFFFFF">They compress the entire training set into a small
codebook of prototype distributions,</font>

23
00:01:41,586 --> 00:01:44,865
<font color="#FFFFFF">and measure how far a new input is from that codebook.</font>

24
00:01:44,945 --> 00:01:47,416
<font color="#FFFFFF">This gives the Distance Aware Bottleneck,</font>

25
00:01:47,496 --> 00:01:52,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">or DAB: a single deterministic model that produces
uncertainty in one forward pass.</font>

26
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:57,931
<font color="#FFFFFF">Along the way they contribute a practical
alternating-minimization training algorithm,</font>

27
00:01:58,011 --> 00:02:02,846
<font color="#FFFFFF">a meta-probabilistic distortion that operates over
distributions of embeddings,</font>

28
00:02:02,926 --> 00:02:08,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a post-hoc variant that adds distance awareness
to large pre-trained feature extractors.</font>

29
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:13,075
<font color="#FFFFFF">DAB builds on the Information Bottleneck framework
but replaces its rate term with an</font>

30
00:02:13,155 --> 00:02:17,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">achievable rate borrowed from rate-distortion
theory with finite cardinality.</font>

31
00:02:17,359 --> 00:02:21,484
<font color="#FFFFFF">Concretely,</font>

32
00:02:21,564 --> 00:02:25,688
<font color="#FFFFFF">the model learns a codebook of centroid
distributions that quantize the encoders of all training points.</font>

33
00:02:25,768 --> 00:02:29,893
<font color="#FFFFFF">The uncertainty of a new example is simply its
expected statistical distance,</font>

34
00:02:29,973 --> 00:02:34,097
<font color="#FFFFFF">here the Kullback-Leibler divergence, from that codebook.</font>

35
00:02:34,177 --> 00:02:38,302
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training alternates between gradient updates of the
encoder and decoder and cheap analytic</font>

36
00:02:38,382 --> 00:02:42,506
<font color="#FFFFFF">updates of the soft assignments and centroids,</font>

37
00:02:42,586 --> 00:02:46,711
<font color="#FFFFFF">echoing the classic Blahut-Arimoto algorithm.</font>

38
00:02:46,791 --> 00:02:50,915
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is closely analogous to a Gaussian Process,</font>

39
00:02:50,995 --> 00:02:55,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the codebook plays the role of inducing
points and statistical distance replaces Euclidean distance.</font>

40
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:59,921
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method is tested across several settings.</font>

41
00:03:00,001 --> 00:03:04,422
<font color="#FFFFFF">On CIFAR-10 as the in-distribution data,</font>

42
00:03:04,502 --> 00:03:08,923
<font color="#FFFFFF">SVHN serves as a far out-of-distribution set and
CIFAR-100 as a harder near out-of-distribution set.</font>

43
00:03:09,003 --> 00:03:13,424
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors also study misclassification prediction
on CIFAR-10 and scale up to ImageNet-1K</font>

44
00:03:13,504 --> 00:03:17,926
<font color="#FFFFFF">with ImageNet-O as the out-of-distribution set.</font>

45
00:03:18,006 --> 00:03:22,427
<font color="#FFFFFF">Notably,</font>

46
00:03:22,507 --> 00:03:26,928
<font color="#FFFFFF">DAB works with a very narrow eight-dimensional
latent bottleneck and just ten distributional codes,</font>

47
00:03:27,008 --> 00:03:31,429
<font color="#FFFFFF">and is compared against strong baselines including
deep ensembles, DDU, DUQ, DUE,</font>

48
00:03:31,509 --> 00:03:35,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">SNGP, and the vanilla variational Information Bottleneck.</font>

49
00:03:36,310 --> 00:03:41,261
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is that DAB outperforms every
baseline on both out-of-distribution tasks.</font>

50
00:03:41,341 --> 00:03:46,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">Trained on CIFAR-10, it reaches an AUROC of 0.986
and AUPRC of 0.994 against SVHN,</font>

51
00:03:46,373 --> 00:03:51,324
<font color="#FFFFFF">and an AUROC of 0.922 and AUPRC of 0.915 against
the harder CIFAR-100,</font>

52
00:03:51,404 --> 00:03:56,355
<font color="#FFFFFF">beating even a five-model deep ensemble.</font>

53
00:03:56,435 --> 00:04:01,386
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially,</font>

54
00:04:01,466 --> 00:04:06,417
<font color="#FFFFFF">it does this in a single forward pass with about
thirty-six and a half million parameters,</font>

55
00:04:06,498 --> 00:04:11,449
<font color="#FFFFFF">versus the ensemble's roughly one hundred and
eighty-two million,</font>

56
00:04:11,529 --> 00:04:16,480
<font color="#FFFFFF">while keeping accuracy on par at about ninety-six percent.</font>

57
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:21,191
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the task of predicting its own mistakes on CIFAR-10,</font>

58
00:04:21,271 --> 00:04:24,479
<font color="#FFFFFF">DAB reaches a calibration AUROC of 0.930.</font>

59
00:04:24,559 --> 00:04:28,889
<font color="#FFFFFF">That nearly closes the gap to a deep ensemble at 0.951,</font>

60
00:04:28,969 --> 00:04:36,428
<font color="#FFFFFF">while dramatically outperforming other single-pass
deterministic methods such as DDU at 0.632,</font>

61
00:04:36,508 --> 00:04:40,117
<font color="#FFFFFF">DUE at 0.856, DUQ at 0.889, and SNGP at 0.897.</font>

62
00:04:40,197 --> 00:04:47,575
<font color="#FFFFFF">A visualization of the learned codebook further
shows each of the ten centroids progressively</font>

63
00:04:47,655 --> 00:04:52,547
<font color="#FFFFFF">attracting test points of a single class as
training proceeds,</font>

64
00:04:52,627 --> 00:04:57,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">confirming that the codes capture meaningful
structure in the data.</font>

65
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:01,539
<font color="#FFFFFF">To summarize the numbers: on CIFAR-10,</font>

66
00:05:01,619 --> 00:05:09,314
<font color="#FFFFFF">DAB achieves a best-in-class OOD AUROC of 0.986
against SVHN and 0.922 against CIFAR-100,</font>

67
00:05:09,394 --> 00:05:13,769
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a misclassification calibration AUROC of 0.930.</font>

68
00:05:13,849 --> 00:05:15,341
<font color="#FFFFFF">At ImageNet scale,</font>

69
00:05:15,421 --> 00:05:22,766
<font color="#FFFFFF">DAB built on a fine-tuned ResNet-50 beats a
five-model ensemble on misclassification,</font>

70
00:05:22,846 --> 00:05:29,405
<font color="#FFFFFF">0.868 versus 0.861, and on out-of-distribution
detection against ImageNet-O,</font>

71
00:05:29,485 --> 00:05:35,257
<font color="#FFFFFF">0.743 versus 0.642, all while using far fewer
trainable parameters,</font>

72
00:05:35,337 --> 00:05:41,110
<font color="#FFFFFF">roughly thirty-six million versus one hundred and
eighteen million.</font>

73
00:05:41,490 --> 00:05:46,587
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is that recasting uncertainty as the
problem of compressing training data</font>

74
00:05:46,667 --> 00:05:51,884
<font color="#FFFFFF">into a learned codebook gives a single
deterministic network a genuine sense of distance</font>

75
00:05:51,964 --> 00:05:53,208
<font color="#FFFFFF">from what it has seen,</font>

76
00:05:53,288 --> 00:05:57,482
<font color="#FFFFFF">letting it match or beat expensive ensembles at a
fraction of the cost.</font>

77
00:05:57,562 --> 00:06:01,575
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because the notion of distance is statistical
rather than geometric,</font>

78
00:06:01,655 --> 00:06:02,839
<font color="#FFFFFF">DAB offers a unified,</font>

79
00:06:02,919 --> 00:06:08,377
<font color="#FFFFFF">Gaussian-Process-like view of uncertainty that
works for both classification and regression,</font>

80
00:06:08,457 --> 00:06:12,470
<font color="#FFFFFF">and can even be attached after the fact to large
pre-trained models.</font>
